    "body": "Ecuadorian President Daniel Noboa meets US Secretary of War Pete Hegseth. ( Image courtesy of Hegseth | X. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 25, 2026\nEcuador has defended its security partnership with the United States after reports that Washington paused joint military operations with the country, where drug gangs are increasingly profiting from illegal gold mining.\nTalks last week with U.S. defence chief Pete Hegseth focused on operations and Ecuador’s security needs, Interior Minister John Reimberg told a radio interviewer Wednesday, Reuters reported. He described the discussions as productive, pointing to an established action plan between the two countries.\n“Some would like to see these relationships, which have yielded so many positive results, damaged,” Reimberg said.\nThe New York Times and The Atlantic reported Tuesday that joint operations had been paused. The Times said the halt was in place by mid-October, amid a U.S. investigation into possible human rights abuses during a Sept. 23 Ecuadorian raid that killed a suspected gang leader. The Pentagon told The Atlantic that U.S. personnel didn’t take part in the raid.\nIllicit extraction\nIllegal mining is among the threats identified in Ecuador’s National Comprehensive Security Plan for 2025–2029. The strategy links illicit mineral extraction to organized crime , money laundering and damage to state institutions.\nPresident Daniel Noboa and Hegseth discussed expanding security co-operation during their Nov. 19 meeting. Noboa did not sign an agreement to continue joint operations, citing domestic opposition ahead of the Nov. 29 local elections, The New York Times reported.\nNoboa announced in March that Ecuador would conduct joint operations with the U.S. and other allies, describing the initiative as “a new phase” in the fight against narco-terrorism and illegal mining. Ecuadorians voted in November 2025 against allowing foreign military bases on national territory.\nCriminal gold\nThe security plan calls for military operations against illegal mining, seizures of supplies and tighter controls over the industry’s logistics chain.\nCriminal groups also use gold mining to launder drug proceeds and finance further operations, according to a June 2025 report by the Global Initiative Against Transnational Organized Crime (GI-TOC).\nThe research describes weak oversight of processing plants in Portovelo and Zaruma that allows illegally mined gold to enter formal export markets.\nLos Lobos extracts gold, extorts miners and controls mine sites and processing plants, the report found. It also identified Guayaquil and Puerto Bolívar as vulnerable points in Ecuador’s gold export chain.\nRegional resistance\nThe Trump administration’s push for regional military co-operation through its Americas Counter Cartel Coalition also faces resistance in Colombia.\nDuring Hegseth’s recent trip, Colombian President Abelardo de la Espriella told Washington he lacked congressional support to authorize joint military operations, The New York Times reported.\nNearly 70% of Ecuador’s protected areas are located in the Napo province, where most illegal miners are active.\nDe La Espriella disputed the report , while Hegseth said on X that the alliance between the two countries was “stronger than ever.”\nAdam Isacson, a security expert at the Washington Office on Latin America, told The New York Times that the Ecuador pause and Hegseth’s reception in the region suggested Washington’s push for joint strikes was “losing momentum.”\nThe Pentagon said it remained “committed to working alongside sovereign partners, including Ecuador, to confront narco-terrorist networks,” in a statement reported by The Atlantic .\n(With files from Reuters)\nPrint\nTAG: Artisanal mining Ecuador Ecuador mining\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold\nREGION: South America United States\nRepublish this article",
